 ## Overview
-Surface-Based Sampling is a ray sampling strategy that restricts 3D point evaluation to a narrow volume surrounding the estimated surface of an object, rather than sampling uniformly across the entire ray segment or free space [S1]. It solves the problem of quality degradation caused by evaluating the neural radiance field in irrelevant regions (free space or occluded areas) by leveraging depth information to focus computational resources on the geometry [S1]. It differs from standard NeRF by eliminating sampling in empty space, and from hierarchical methods like [Coarse-to-Fine NeRF](3406-coarse-to-fine-nerf.md) by using explicit depth priors rather than a secondary network to determine sampling density [S1].
+Surface-based sampling is a strategy that restricts 3D point sampling to the area close to the surface of an object, rather than sampling throughout the entire volume or free space [S1]. It solves the problem of quality degradation caused by standard NeRF methods sampling in irrelevant regions such as free space or occluded areas [S1]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Pixel Region and Depth Guided Ray Sampling](8866-pixel-region-and-depth-guided-ray-sampling.md) and [Truncated Depth-Based Sampling](10613-truncated-depth-based-sampling.md) by explicitly defining a fixed-width band around an estimated depth value $d$ for stratified sampling, rather than using pixel-region heuristics or hard truncation limits [S1].
 
